# positional — every command declares its positional contract, so a blank
# identifier, an unexpected positional and an unknown subcommand are all
# refused before the command runs (INVARIANTS.md H-22).
#
# A VIOLATION surface: internal/cmd's undeclaredPositionalSites lists only the
# commands whose `Args` is not one of the five constructors in
# internal/cmd/args.go. Empty is the goal, and it is empty. The extractor is
# guarded against silently matching nothing by
# TestPositionalExtractorFlagsAnUndeclaredCommand, which feeds it a tree
# containing a command with no Args and a command with a bare cobra validator
# and requires both to be flagged — not by a non-empty check, which a surface
# whose whole purpose is to reach zero can never have.
#
# It was not empty. Cobra decided the contract for every command that did not
# state one, and its defaults are all permissive in the same direction:
#
#   - `Args == nil` is ArbitraryArgs, so `ent ls sensor` returned the same
#     unfiltered listing as `ent ls` and dropped the argument in silence — the
#     most plausible mistake an LLM caller makes with that command.
#   - `ExactArgs(1)` counts the argument without looking at it, so `auto show ''`
#     and `auto delete ''` reached a resolver whose four equality tests a
#     restored ghost satisfies with its own empty config id. The `auto delete`
#     dry run printed a plan to delete a real automation nobody had named; one
#     `--confirm` from a destructive write against the wrong object. `device
#     show ''` and `area create ''` were the same defect one registry over.
#   - `legacyArgs` refuses an unknown command for the root and returns nil for
#     every other group, and cobra answers a group with no Run by printing help
#     and exiting 0 — so `hactl helper set` and `hactl dash frobnicate` told a
#     caller checking $? that they had worked. Setting Args on a help-only group
#     does not fix it: `execute` returns flag.ErrHelp before ValidateArgs runs,
#     which is why `family` installs a RunE as well.
#
# Two behavioural gates quantify the same rule over the live tree without a
# ledger, because their failures are never debt: TestNoCommandAcceptsABlankPositional
# (every command, one/two/three blank arguments) and
# TestEveryFamilyRefusesAnUnknownSubcommand (every command that holds
# subcommands). This file exists for the structural half — a command that
# declares no contract at all — which is the one that can be added silently.
#ceiling 0
